@page "/about"
@using SplatStudio.Infrastructure.Splatting
@inject GpuSplatEngine Gpu
@inject SplattingOptions Splatting

<PageTitle>How SplatStudio works</PageTitle>

@* The README is blunt about what this technique can and cannot do. That caveat
   belongs in the product too — someone deciding whether to trust a result should
   not have to find it on GitHub. *@
<section class="page-section prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">How it works</p>
    <h1>What you are actually looking at</h1>

    <p>
        Real 3D Gaussian splatting is trained from many photos of the same subject shot
        from different angles. A pipeline like COLMAP recovers where each camera was,
        and then millions of Gaussians are fitted against all those views over hours of
        GPU time. A single flat photo does not contain that information, and no amount
        of clever code puts it back.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
        SplatStudio does something smaller and honest about it: it estimates depth from
        one image and builds a real, orbitable point cloud out of that estimate. Not
        photogrammetry — closer to turning a photo into a snow globe.
    </div>

    <h2>The estimate, step by step</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>The image is downscaled to fit the splat budget (currently
            <span class="mono">@ActiveBudget.ToString("N0")</span> points).</li>
        <li>Each pixel gets a pseudo-depth: bright pixels and central pixels are pulled
            toward the camera, dark and peripheral ones pushed away.</li>
        <li>That depth field is blurred slightly so the surface reads as continuous
            rather than per-pixel noise.</li>
        <li>Every surviving pixel becomes one Gaussian splat, positioned in that depth
            field and coloured from the source image.</li>
        <li>The result is written as a 32-bytes-per-point binary file and rendered in
            your browser with WebGL.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What it does well, and where it breaks</h2>
    <p>
        One clear subject against a plain background — a portrait, an object on a
        backdrop — looks convincing, because for that composition "bright and central"
        genuinely correlates with "near". Orbit maybe thirty degrees off-axis and the
        illusion holds.
    </p>
    <p>
        It fails in ways worth seeing. A ring gets its hole filled in, because the
        centre-weighted prior insists the middle is close. Flat scenes stay flat. Nothing
        recovers geometry hidden behind the subject, because that geometry was never
        photographed. The gallery deliberately keeps those cases in.
    </p>

    <h2>Where it runs</h2>
    <p>
        The depth field and the splat emission are the parallel part of the job, so they
        run as GPU compute kernels when a device is available and fall back to the CPU
        when one isn't.
    </p>

    <div class="glass-panel scene-telemetry">
        <div class="telemetry-item">
            <span class="telemetry-label">Configured engine</span>
            <span class="telemetry-value">@EngineLabel</span>
        </div>
        <div class="telemetry-item">
            <span class="telemetry-label">Compute device</span>
            <span class="telemetry-value @(Gpu.IsAvailable ? "is-near" : "")">
                @(Gpu.IsAvailable ? Gpu.DeviceName : "CPU only")
            </span>
        </div>
        <div class="telemetry-item">
            <span class="telemetry-label">Splat budget</span>
            <span class="telemetry-value">@ActiveBudget.ToString("N0")</span>
        </div>
        <div class="telemetry-item">
            <span class="telemetry-label">File size</span>
            <span class="telemetry-value">@((ActiveBudget * 32 / (1024.0 * 1024.0)).ToString("0.0")) MB</span>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h2>Swapping in the real thing</h2>
    <p>
        The engine sits behind a single interface, so a genuine multi-view backend —
        nerfstudio, gsplat, a hosted service — can replace it without touching the
        gallery, the viewer, or the storage layer. That was the point of keeping the
        approximation behind a port rather than wiring it through the app.
    </p>
</section>

@code {
    private bool UsingGpu =>
        Gpu.IsAvailable && string.Equals(Splatting.Engine, "Gpu", StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase);

    private int ActiveBudget => UsingGpu ? Splatting.GpuMaxPoints : Splatting.MaxPoints;

    /// <summary>Reads back the configuration value the way a person would say it.</summary>
    private string EngineLabel => Splatting.Engine?.ToLowerInvariant() switch
    {
        "gpu" => Gpu.IsAvailable ? "GPU heuristic" : "GPU heuristic (no device — using CPU)",
        "externalapi" => "External API",
        _ => "CPU heuristic"
    };
}
